# Using the cross-project knowledge base (~/dev/kb)

Harness-neutral. Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode and pi all point here; this file is the
one home for the rule, so edit it here rather than in any harness's global config.
The wiring itself — which file each harness loads, how the stanza is stamped and
verified, how to add a harness — is documented in README.md, "Harnesses — how the kb
hooks into the agents".

`~/dev/kb` holds durable, evidence-linked findings that outlive the project they were
learned in. `INDEX.md` lists every note; `README.md` is the contract.

## Read before deriving

Before deriving, re-measuring, or asserting a cross-project claim — simulator
semantics, vendor-data semantics, device behaviour, measurement technique, host/WAF
access recipes, agent-harness behaviour — check `~/dev/kb/INDEX.md` for a note and read
it. Recomputation is not new evidence. A `tombstoned` note is a *result* (this was
tried and falsified), not an absence.

## Write when it generalises

A finding that would change how another project is built, measured, or simulated
belongs here:

- One claim per note, in a topic directory, filename = kebab-case claim slug.
- `bin/kb new <topic> <slug>` scaffolds it at `status: unverified`.
- Frontmatter `evidence:` is a list of **structured locators**, one kind per entry:
  `repo:` (path must resolve) · `code:` +path+at · `manual:` +version+section/page ·
  `datasheet:` +vendor+rev/page/date · `vendordoc:` +vendor+date/rev/url ·
  `standard:` +amendment/year/clause · `paper:` +where · `doi:` · `url:` +retrieved ·
  `measurement:` +where · `observation:` +where. The table in README.md is the
  reference. Free text is rejected on a verified note — if you cannot name a locator,
  the note is `unverified`, and that is a legitimate thing for it to be.
- `status: verified` requires evidence that validates; that promotion is an act, not a
  default, and it means a person or reviewing agent read the source.
- Project-bound facts stay in the project repo. When a topic's canonical material
  already lives in a project, write a `kind: pointer` note naming it — never a copy.
- Correct by **tombstoning**: `status: tombstoned` plus a dated correction on top.
  Never delete, never silently edit a falsified claim.
- Never: credentials, keys, private network details. Licensed PDFs get a pointer and a
  hash, not redistribution.

## Maintenance

`bin/kb check [--staged] | index | new <topic> <slug> | stale [days] | harness [--live]
| stamp | selftest | install-hook`.

The versioned `.githooks/pre-commit` (activate once with `bin/kb install-hook`) runs
`selftest` and `check --staged`, validating the blobs git will actually record. Commit
kb changes as you make them, and run `bin/kb index` + stage `INDEX.md` alongside any
note you add — the gate will not silently regenerate it for you.

`status: verified` means **a person or a reviewing agent checked the source**. The tool
only checks that a locator was declared and that any local path in it resolves; it
cannot read the source. Never promote a note to `verified` because `kb check` passed.

If your harness has a memory feature, a memory that duplicates a kb note should be a
recall hook only (`Canonical: ~/dev/kb/<note>`), with the claim maintained here.

