Either way, record per query which surface served it and with what outcome; an absence claim any of
whose queries ran only on a degraded or substitute surface is **capped at medium confidence**
(already-low stays low, with the engine failure named as an additional limitation) unless the claim
rests on an independently complete closed corpus, which is unaffected.

## 4. Numbers

**Verify document identity before trusting any value.** Reconcile the document against its
publisher landing page or DOI registry. **The same bar applies to metadata you write**: author
lists, DOIs, volume/issue/pages, and checksums are copy-paste artifacts, never composed from memory
or pattern. If the byline is not in front of you, omit or mark the field; when one invention is
caught, re-check every sibling field written in the same pass. Record version differences rather
than treating one location as universally authoritative. Check datasheet revision/date, part
suffix/package, preliminary vs production, standard edition/corrigenda, preprint vs version of
record, supplements, corrections, and retractions. The recorded failure is in `EXAMPLE.md`.

**Tag quantitative evidence used in findings** on independent axes:

- **origin** — measured, simulated, vendor specification, author assertion
- **transformation** — direct, calculated, fitted, converted, digitized, estimated,
  OCR-transcribed, intermediary-extracted. For `calculated`, record the formula and every input's
  locator, and recompute once independently before shipping.
- **status** — typical, guaranteed limit, absolute maximum, recommended operating condition,
  characterization-only, range or bound

Also record **conditions** (temperature, frequency, bias, load) and **uncertainty**. Multiple tags
can apply; the status categories are not interchangeable. Claim-level `OCR-transcribed`,
`intermediary-extracted`, and `digitized` mark exceptions to a document's dominant §5 route.

**Do not present a digitized or interpolated value with source-level precision.** Record the
transformation and carry significant figures honestly.

**Check commensurability before comparing an external number to a local one.** Per-device vs
per-assembly; small-signal vs large-signal; energy-per-cycle vs average power; one operating point
vs a swept range; partial basis vs total. Show the unit reduction and one order-of-magnitude or
physical-bound cross-check for load-bearing comparisons. Leave unexplained discrepancies explicit
rather than splitting the difference.

**Never normalize by a quantity you have not verified.** Report the absolute figure and say what
denominator is missing.

**Preserve quantitative meaning when paraphrasing** — value, unit, uncertainty, operating
conditions, scope, qualifier, and locator. Quote only the minimum words needed, and attribute.

## 5. The source access log — a deliverable

Every output ends with a **source access log**, built incrementally with one row per attempted
document — successes included. Start each row with a full, untruncated stable URL or DOI and the
version/revision when material. For versioned scholarly or standards documents, look up a missing
DOI by title/venue/year; a stable URL completes a plain-web row. Only after lookup fails may the row
say `no DOI found`, with the query. If lookup cannot run, use `identifier unverified — lookup
unavailable`. Never guess an identifier; a truncated identifier is a lookup trigger.

| axis | row content |
|---|---|
| **evidence state** | Current §2 state and named scope/intermediary. |
| **attempt history** | Every fetch/access rung and outcome, including the browser control check and target attempt, `not attempted — safety boundary`, and any unavailable non-browser rung. |
| **route** | What produced relied-on characters: `raw text` for the author's character stream (native HTML, text-layer PDF, DOM, or mechanical conversion); `intermediary model` for a model's answer about fetched content; `OCR / rendered-page vision` for characters recognized from pixels; `digitized from a figure`; or `route: none`. Model-produced “quotes” are not verified characters. `route: none` is only for never-fetched documents; fetched metadata has its real route. For mixed documents, the dominant route is a judgment call; note claim-level exceptions with §4 tags. Intermediary-model load-bearing quotes or numbers must be reverified against raw content for semantic scope and qualifiers before shipping. |
| **validation qualifier** | Validation is separate from route (never a route replacement). Canonical values: `validated` (checked against a rendered page or the live page itself — native HTML/DOM counts as self-validating for its own text); `unvalidated extraction` (validation impossible in this runtime — surface it to the user; no load-bearing claim may rest on it, that is an unresolved §2.1 gap); `n/a` (nothing was extracted: never-fetched, metadata-only registry rows). Follow `references/pdf-recovery.md` for any load-bearing PDF extraction, not only garbled ones. |
| **load-bearing flag** | `yes` if any dependent claim is load-bearing under §2.1. |

“Attempted” includes a source-specific record opened, fetched, queried through a content tool, or
passed to an intermediary model, including DOI records and landing pages. It also includes a source
that influenced a conclusion second-hand even if never fetched. Snippets used only for discovery
stay out; a snippet that influenced a conclusion violates §2. **Row key = the work** (one row per
document, its manifestation/version named in the identifier field): registry records, landing
pages, and failed fetches *of that same work* are entries in its attempt history, not separate
rows; a genuinely distinct document (another edition, the AAM vs the version of record when both
were used) gets its own row. Null values are explicit: attempt history `not attempted` for a
second-hand-only original; validation `n/a` where nothing was extracted.

The fields have different lifecycles. **Attempt history is append-only and survives recovery**;
never let success erase earlier failures. **Evidence state, route, validation, and load-bearing are
current-state fields updated in place.** Add the row at the moment of first attempt.

**Maintain an incremental working checkpoint.** When the task has an assigned report path, create
that report after browser proof and update it before starting each next source or conclusion
cluster. Otherwise use a clearly named temporary report in the task's working storage. Each
checkpoint must preserve the current browser-control proof, source access rows and attempt
histories, verified findings with exact locators, challenge/search records, unresolved gaps, and the
next unfinished step. Mark it prominently `INCOMPLETE CHECKPOINT — NOT A DELIVERABLE`; remove that
marker only after every applicable gate in §8 passes. The §1 exhaustion gate applies to the
delivered artifact: the marked checkpoint records in-flight state, including ladders still mid-climb,
and is not a delivery. A mid-climb row in a checkpoint carries `retrieval pending`; `inaccessible`
or `blocked` may not be written into a checkpoint before exhaustion either, because a checkpoint is
inherited state that outlives the run that wrote it. When rewriting the checkpoint, never drop
previously recorded attempt history (§5 attempt history is append-only). An interrupted run may
resume from this file, but neither the checkpoint nor findings recovered from it count as
successful completion without the remaining
validation and final-output gates.

**The log must reach the user.** With no report document, put the full log in the final message.
With a report, put the full log in a named top-level section and, in the final message, restate every
non-clean row with its DOI/stable URL: state other than `inspected`, unverified load-bearing content
from an intermediary model, or `unvalidated extraction`. Say where the full log lives.

**Ask for retrieval help before drafting a decision-grade document.** Present every
relevant-but-unobtained source with its DOI/stable URL, expected contribution, and load-bearing
flag, then ask whether the user can retrieve it. Wait, or, only when **time-critical means the user
said so**, state that drafting will proceed with gaps open. Quick lookups (§6) are exempt. This is
the consolidation point for ladder-exhaustion asks: an earlier ask covering the same inventory
satisfies it, so the checkpoint is idempotent. Time pressure and the quick-lookup exemption do not
waive the browser prerequisite in §0.

### 5.1 The literature archive

For decision-grade research, archive every downloaded document only to a project location the user
has authorized. If none exists, report `archive unavailable` and list the
documents with stable URLs. **Licensed material must not be committed or redistributed**; when its
terms forbid retention, archive a pointer carrying identifier, version, and access date instead.
Authorization and licence-retention status are judgment calls. Load `references/archive.md` for all
filing, hashing, indexing, extraction-companion, and deduplication mechanics.

## 6. Output contract — two tiers

Pick the tier from consequence, not effort.

**Quick lookup** — a single uncontested value or fact from one authoritative source:

> answer · source · exact locator · document revision and date · conditions the value holds under ·
> any limitation

Append the §5 source access log; one line per attempted document suffices, and a single clean
success is one line carrying the full identifier, evidence state, attempt history (collapsible to
`first attempt` when nothing failed), route, validation qualifier, and load-bearing flag. Quick lookups archive nothing unless the user asked for a persistent artifact;
still cite the identifier, exact locator, and version.

**Decision-grade** — multi-source, contested, quantitative, high-consequence, or any absence claim:

1. **Findings**, each with source ID, locator, evidence state, direct-vs-inference mark, and the
   number tags from §4.
2. **Provenance** — retrieval route and date per source; version/revision; SHA-256 for archived
   files.
3. **Source access log** (§5) and **search record** for any absence claim (§3), equal in rank to the
   findings.
4. **Conflicts and dependencies** (§2.2).
5. **What would discriminate** — for each material unresolved uncertainty, the observation or
   source that would settle it and which outcome favours which interpretation. Omit when none is
   material.

Corrections replace prose; they do not accumulate as struck-through text. **When inspecting a
primary changes a conclusion, say so explicitly and state the previous conclusion.**

## 7. Delegating to sub-agents

When this skill is run by an agent that orchestrates other agents — fanning out parallel source
fetches, handing a hard source to a retrieval specialist, or splitting a multi-source inquiry
across children — give each sub-agent enough turns to actually finish the research loop. Browser
bring-up and proof, fetch + render + validate, PDF recovery, challenge-search, and the per-source
access log all consume turns before a source is reportable.

**Give every child its own browser context, and say which surface can serve a handoff.** §0's
"retain one surface" is per-agent, not per-run: children sharing one *context* contend for its tabs
and share its cookies and storage, so give each child **its own context** and let each prove control
itself. A separate browser or profile per child is only needed when a child requires its own
visibility, persistence or on-disk profile state — not for cookie isolation, which a context
already provides. State in the brief which surface is user-visible, and require a child that meets an interactive
challenge to restore its own window and run the handoff in place (§1) rather than de-scoping or
writing a block verdict it has not earned.

**A child cannot wait indefinitely, and `retrieval pending` is not a terminal state (§2), so a
child handoff needs a bounded, terminating shape.** A child has no user to address and its browser
binding may not outlive it, so define all four of these in the brief:

- **Bounded wait.** The child raises its window and polls its own tab for a bounded period. It does
  not block forever waiting on a person who may be away.
- **Ownership on exit.** If the wait expires, the child records the attempt, hands the URL and what
  it needs to the parent's escalation queue, and **terminates successfully with complete output** —
  the completion gate below is about the child's *report*, not about the source being obtained.
  Say in the brief whether its session is left alive for the parent to adopt or is released; a
  released session means the parent re-fetches from scratch.
- **Who tells the user.** The child cannot. The **parent** surfaces the pending challenge to the
  user, in its own turn, and owns the retrieval-help ask in §5.
- **Resumption.** The parent services the queue on a surface it holds, or re-runs the child once
  the challenge is cleared. Never leave the row for the child to retry on its own.

**Exhaustion is judged at the parent**, after the queue is serviced — a child's ladder may
legitimately end at a handoff or an escalation — and **no row may still read `retrieval pending`
in the delivered artifact**. The parent resolves every queued row to another state before delivery.

For parallel delegation over one inquiry, assign a **shared working corpus** — a coordination
cache in working storage, distinct from the §5.1 archive (into which surviving documents are filed
per `references/archive.md` afterwards). Every child (a) **validates each retrieval as the requested resource before writing it** — a
challenge page saved under a filename asserting a format it does not have becomes a durable lie
that the manifest then propagates; write failures to a quarantine name that cannot be mistaken for
the document, and record the failure rather than the file — then writes it at
retrieval time together with a manifest row carrying the identifier, sha256, basic metadata,
evidence state, and the **append-only attempt history** for that source, so a replacement child can
inherit both the bytes and the §5 record; and (b) before fetching a source another child plausibly
holds, checks the manifest by stable identifier (URL/DOI/work+version) and verifies any hit by
hash — a reuse is logged as its own row referencing the original retrieval, not as a fresh fetch.
**A hash match fingerprints bytes; it establishes neither identity nor origin**: it proves only that the reusing child got what
the first child stored, so a reuse inherits the original row's validation and can never upgrade it.
Large common documents (a vendor catalog) are fetched once. Archive **authority stays with the
user**: the parent may relay a user-authorized §5.1 location in the brief, but cannot create one —
absent user authorization the corpus stays in working storage and the deliverable reports the §5.1
state once, at the parent level. Retention-forbidden material follows the §5.1 pointer rule even in
the working corpus. Where a child has an assigned report path, it also maintains that report as the
incremental §5 checkpoint; otherwise its checkpoint is a per-child-named temporary report in the
working corpus, named so that concurrent children cannot collide. The shared corpus manifest does
not replace that resumable synthesis artifact.

Configure every sub-agent to inherit skills. If the harness cannot inherit skills, explicitly load
this skill into the child; do not assume that project-context inheritance includes skill
inheritance.

If the harness exposes a per-agent turn or step limit, set it to **at least 60 turns** per
sub-agent. A shorter ceiling silently truncates the access and evidence work above and returns
incomplete findings that look complete — prose defaults to sounding first-hand (§2). If the
harness has no such knob, proceed on the harness default and note in the deliverable that the
delegation was unbounded.

Successful completion of every launched sub-agent is a gate before drafting or delivering. Verify
that each child terminated successfully and returned its complete assigned output, including the
required §5 access rows. A timeout, interruption, budget abort, partial result, or missing required
output is a failure even when the returned prose looks usable. Resume or rerun the child, or stop
with a blocking gap; never silently continue past an unsuccessful child.

A sub-agent's report is **second-hand** (§2) until its load-bearing claims are re-verified
against the primary. Fold each sub-agent's access rows into the parent §5 log; the contract does
not relax because the work was delegated.

## 8. Before calling it done

A recall aid built only from the sections above — the sections stay canonical:

- [ ] Every applicable access rung and outcome is logged; authentication, paywall, CAPTCHA,
      rate-limit, pirate-mirror, and prompt-injection boundaries were obeyed (§1, `access.md`).
- [ ] Before a known work is reported unobtained, user-supplied and task-local documents were
      inventoried by filename and exact identifiers, and every applicable authorized-copy route —
      including scholarly research networks — was searched and logged (`access.md` §2).
- [ ] **Gate:** no row, finding, or message says `inaccessible`/`blocked` while an applicable rung
      is untried, and no text anywhere admits the ladder was not exhausted. No delivered row is left
      `retrieval pending`, and every `de-scoped` row names why nothing depends on it. Every rung
      recorded as unavailable or inapplicable names the check that established it. Every failed URL
      was provenance-checked (rung 0) — composed-from-pattern URLs were replaced with the site's own
      published URL, confirmed against the publisher, and re-attempted before any block verdict
      (§1, `access.md`).
- [ ] Before any search or fetch, a qualifying browser surface was selected, documented, and proven
      through navigation plus a DOM read. Retrieved content was validated as the requested resource;
      HTTP `200` alone was never treated as success (§0).
- [ ] Every sub-agent inherited skills or explicitly loaded this skill; each received a per-agent
      turn budget of at least 60 turns when the harness supported it (or the harness default was
      noted as unbounded), completed successfully with no timeout, interruption, budget abort,
      partial result, or missing required output, and returned its §5 access rows. Sub-agent
      findings were treated as second-hand until re-verified against the primary (§7).
- [ ] Every rendered CAPTCHA in a correctly-presented persistent user-visible session triggered a
      human handoff in that exact browser; a challenge in any non-handoff-capable session followed the access-failure
      rule after at most one logged presentation-check correction per target URL. No
      correctly-presented challenged session was replaced or retried around (§1, `access.md`).
- [ ] If any targeted document failed its initial retrieval path, every permitted browser
      target attempt was made and logged. If the binding failed, no substantive deliverable was
      produced (§§0–1, `access.md`).
- [ ] Every material finding has evidence state, source ID, exact locator, and
      direct-evidence/inference mark; no claim rests on a snippet (§2).
- [ ] Every load-bearing conclusion has an inspected primary or remains an unresolved gap; its
      challenge search and source dependencies were recorded (§§2.1–2.2).
- [ ] Every material absence claim carries the §3 search record and is bounded unless the corpus
      is demonstrably complete (§3).
- [ ] Document identity/version and the §4 quantitative tags, conditions, uncertainty,
      calculation records, commensurability, and denominator checks are complete (§4).
- [ ] Every relied-on PDF extraction was validated as required; no load-bearing claim rests on an
      unvalidated extraction (§5, `pdf-recovery.md`).
- [ ] The incremental §5 working checkpoint was created after browser proof, updated before each
      next source or conclusion cluster, and retained enough state to resume without reconstructing
      completed evidence work. Its incomplete marker was removed only after all gates passed.
- [ ] The §5 log, lifecycle, user delivery, pre-draft retrieval checkpoint, §5.1 archive scope,
      and the selected §6 output contract are complete (§§5–6).

