# When a PDF will not give you usable text

## The policy

Text, OCR, and rendered pixels are **complementary representations**. Making any one of them the
universal default is the mistake.

1. **Extract text first, then validate it** — do not assume it worked. Compare the title and at
   least one relevant body passage against a rendered page.
2. **Use text (or OCR, for scans) for discovery** — locating the sections, terms, tables, and page
   numbers that matter. This is what scales over a 300-page document.
3. **Render only the candidate pages** — the evidence pages, figures, equations, and tables you will
   actually cite. Not the whole document.
4. **Visually verify every load-bearing number, table row, or topology** against the rendered page
   before citing it.
5. **Increase resolution or crop** when typography is dense: subscripts, plot legends, and
   multi-column tables are where both OCR and vision fail quietly.

Rendering the entire document is expensive in latency, disk, and context, and 150 dpi is not always
enough. Vision has its own failure modes — transposing table rows, missing footnotes, misreading
scientific notation and sign — so it validates evidence, it does not replace extraction.

**A garbled extraction you still cite is a render trigger, not a caveat opportunity.** Citing "only
the values that extracted cleanly" from a broken table feels compliant and is the documented path
to a wrong claim: measured 2026-08-18, a diversity table whose pdftotext layout was garbled was
cited with exactly that caveat; the claim anchored to it (which of two effects was larger) shipped
inverted and was falsified by the first 200-dpi raster of the page — locating and rendering the one
page took under a minute. When rendering tooling exists, a garbled table's page gets rendered
before any value or comparison from it is cited; the `unvalidated extraction` **validation qualifier** (it is a qualifier on the row, never a
replacement for the route — the route stays `raw text`/`OCR` per `SKILL.md` §5) is
legitimate only when no rendering tooling is available in the runtime. It never permits a
load-bearing claim to ship: that remains an unresolved `SKILL.md` §2.1 gap, not a caveat.

**Figures and equations are the exception that justifies rendering at all.** Where the claim is
about a *structure* — a circuit topology, a block diagram, which branch an element sits in, the
shape of a curve — text extraction cannot answer it even when it works perfectly, because it returns
labels without relations. Render those pages.

**Record the extraction route in the source access log** (`SKILL.md` §5): whether the document was
read as raw text or required rendering/OCR, so nobody later trusts a text extraction of it — and so
every number quoted from it visibly carries the OCR transcription risk. If the validation step
above was impossible (no rendering or OCR tooling in the runtime), record `unvalidated extraction`
in the log as the row's validation qualifier, alongside its real route — never silently claim a
clean validated extraction.

## Diagnosing the failure mode

| symptom | cause | response |
|---|---|---|
| words run together, ligatures or diacritics missing | encoding fine, tokenization lost | search case-insensitively without word boundaries; text is usable |
| letters uniformly displaced by a constant | substitution-cipher font encoding | see below |
| control characters, CID glyph ids, nothing recoverable | symbolic font with no `ToUnicode` map | render; OCR if bulk text needed |
| empty text, uniform page images | scanned document | OCR for discovery, render for evidence |
| text present but nonsense only in *part* of the file | multiple embedded fonts, some broken | do not conclude from a grep miss — render the section |

## Structured conversion with Docling

Docling is an **optional post-retrieval extraction rung**, not an access or validation rung. Use it
only after the requested PDF has been obtained and validated as the real document. It does not
bypass a WAF, paywall, CAPTCHA, authentication boundary, or the mandatory browser work in
`SKILL.md` §§0–1.

Try Docling when `pdftotext` loses reading order or table structure, or when structured Markdown or
JSON would materially improve discovery or reproducibility. Keep `pdftotext` as the first attempt
for clean, short, born-digital PDFs: Docling has a substantial model, startup, memory, and processing
cost. For a PDF with a trustworthy text layer, start with `--no-ocr`; enable OCR only when the
document actually contains scans or missing/broken text regions. The CLI defaults to embedded
base64 images, which can inflate research artifacts by orders of magnitude, so use
`--image-export-mode placeholder` unless image files are explicitly needed.

```sh
# Born-digital PDF: preserve structure without needlessly invoking OCR.
docling convert in.pdf --from pdf --to md --to json --no-ocr \
  --image-export-mode placeholder --output out

# Scanned or regionally broken PDF: leave OCR enabled and record the recognized-pixel route.
docling convert in.pdf --from pdf --to md --to json \
  --image-export-mode placeholder --output out
```

Confirm the installed version's syntax with `docling convert --help`; options and models move. If a
full conversion is unnecessary, use `--page-range` for the candidate pages.

**Structured output is still only discovery material.** A plausible Markdown table can contain a
wrong decimal glyph, merged header, shifted cell, duplicated caption, or sidebar inserted into the
reading order; OCR can transpose otherwise convincing values. Validate every load-bearing table
cell, number, equation, figure relation, and qualifier against the rendered source page exactly as
required above. Docling does not upgrade an evidence state or create a new route category: record
the characters as `raw text` when they came from the PDF character stream and as
`OCR / rendered-page vision` when they were recognized from pixels; note mixed or claim-level
exceptions. Table cell grouping and reading order are inferred structure and therefore require
visual checking even when the characters themselves are raw text.

If Docling output is retained as a research artifact, record the Docling version and material
options (`--ocr`/`--no-ocr`, page range, table mode, and image mode), and follow
`references/archive.md` for extraction companions and licence constraints.

## Substitution-cipher encodings

Rare, but they produce output that reads as corruption and can be recovered losslessly. A uniform
letter displacement (`WHFKQLTXHV` for `TECHNIQUES` is a shift of 3) can be undone by trying all 26
shifts and scoring against a dictionary.

Two cautions, both of which have bitten:

- **A file can mix encodings.** One font may decode cleanly while another in the same document does
  not, so a successful decode of the abstract is no guarantee for the body.
- **Dictionary scoring can make partially-corrupted text look plausible.** Always verify recovered
  text visually against a rendered page before quoting it.

Most importantly: **a grep miss in a partially-broken file is not evidence the document lacks the
topic.** That inference is the same error as treating a blocked fetch as absence. Render and look.

## Commands

Check what is present before relying on any of these; availability differs per machine and versions
move. Do not treat a tool inventory as a fact about the environment.

```sh
pdftotext -layout in.pdf out.txt          # first attempt; then validate
pdfinfo in.pdf                            # page count and page size, to plan rendering
# locate the page that holds a known caption/heading, then render only it:
for p in $(seq 1 $(pdfinfo in.pdf | awk '/^Pages/{print $2}')); do
  pdftotext -f $p -l $p in.pdf - | grep -q "Alpha diversity indices" && echo "page $p"; done
pdftoppm -png -r 150 -f 4 -l 6 in.pdf pg  # render ONLY pages 4-6
pdftoppm -png -r 300 -f 4 -l 4 in.pdf pg  # higher dpi for dense tables or small type
```

For bulk searchable text over a scanned or CID-broken document:

```sh
ocrmypdf --force-ocr --sidecar out.txt in.pdf out.pdf   # --redo-ocr if a bad layer exists
tesseract page-4.png - --psm 1                          # single page, quick
```

Platform OCR engines (for example Apple Vision via `shortcuts run`, or the equivalent on other
systems) are sometimes better than Tesseract on typeset multi-column papers and sometimes worse.
Compare on the document at hand rather than carrying a preference between engines.

