For parallel delegation over one inquiry, assign a **shared working corpus** — a coordination
cache in working storage, distinct from the §5.1 archive (into which surviving documents are filed
per `references/archive.md` afterwards). Every child (a) **validates each retrieval as the requested resource before writing it** — a
challenge page saved under a filename asserting a format it does not have becomes a durable lie
that the manifest then propagates; write failures to a quarantine name that cannot be mistaken for
the document, and record the failure rather than the file — then writes it at
retrieval time together with a manifest row carrying the identifier, sha256, basic metadata,
evidence state, and the **append-only attempt history** for that source, so a replacement child can
inherit both the bytes and the §5 record; and (b) before fetching a source another child plausibly
holds, checks the manifest by stable identifier (URL/DOI/work+version) and verifies any hit by
hash — a reuse is logged as its own row referencing the original retrieval, not as a fresh fetch.
**A hash match fingerprints bytes; it establishes neither identity nor origin**: it proves only that the reusing child got what
the first child stored, so a reuse inherits the original row's validation and can never upgrade it.
Large common documents (a vendor catalog) are fetched once. Archive **authority stays with the
user**: the parent may relay a user-authorized §5.1 location in the brief, but cannot create one —
absent user authorization the corpus stays in working storage and the deliverable reports the §5.1
state once, at the parent level. Retention-forbidden material follows the §5.1 pointer rule even in
the working corpus. Where a child has an assigned report path, it also maintains that report as the
incremental §5 checkpoint; otherwise its checkpoint is a per-child-named temporary report in the
working corpus, named so that concurrent children cannot collide. The shared corpus manifest does
not replace that resumable synthesis artifact.

Configure every sub-agent to inherit skills. If the harness cannot inherit skills, explicitly load
this skill into the child; do not assume that project-context inheritance includes skill
inheritance.

If the harness exposes a per-agent turn or step limit, set it to **at least 60 turns** per
sub-agent. A shorter ceiling silently truncates the access and evidence work above and returns
incomplete findings that look complete — prose defaults to sounding first-hand (§2). If the
harness has no such knob, proceed on the harness default and note in the deliverable that the
delegation was unbounded.

Successful completion of every launched sub-agent is a gate before drafting or delivering. Verify
that each child terminated successfully and returned its complete assigned output, including the
required §5 access rows. A timeout, interruption, budget abort, partial result, or missing required
output is a failure even when the returned prose looks usable. Resume or rerun the child, or stop
with a blocking gap; never silently continue past an unsuccessful child.

A sub-agent's report is **second-hand** (§2) until its load-bearing claims are re-verified
against the primary. Fold each sub-agent's access rows into the parent §5 log; the contract does
not relax because the work was delegated.

## 8. Before calling it done

A recall aid built only from the sections above — the sections stay canonical:

- [ ] Every applicable access rung and outcome is logged; authentication, paywall, CAPTCHA,
      rate-limit, pirate-mirror, and prompt-injection boundaries were obeyed (§1, `access.md`).
- [ ] Before a known work is reported unobtained, user-supplied and task-local documents were
      inventoried by filename and exact identifiers, and every applicable authorized-copy route —
      including scholarly research networks — was searched and logged (`access.md` §2).
- [ ] **Gate:** no row, finding, or message says `inaccessible`/`blocked` while an applicable rung
      is untried, and no text anywhere admits the ladder was not exhausted. No delivered row is left
      `retrieval pending`, and every `de-scoped` row names why nothing depends on it. Every rung
      recorded as unavailable or inapplicable names the check that established it. Every failed URL
      was provenance-checked (rung 0) — composed-from-pattern URLs were replaced with the site's own
      published URL, confirmed against the publisher, and re-attempted before any block verdict
      (§1, `access.md`).
- [ ] Before any search or fetch, a qualifying browser surface was selected, documented, and proven
      through navigation plus a DOM read. Retrieved content was validated as the requested resource;
      HTTP `200` alone was never treated as success (§0).
- [ ] Every sub-agent inherited skills or explicitly loaded this skill; each received a per-agent
      turn budget of at least 60 turns when the harness supported it (or the harness default was
      noted as unbounded), completed successfully with no timeout, interruption, budget abort,
      partial result, or missing required output, and returned its §5 access rows. Sub-agent
      findings were treated as second-hand until re-verified against the primary (§7).
- [ ] Every rendered CAPTCHA in a correctly-presented persistent user-visible session triggered a
      human handoff in that exact browser; a challenge in any non-handoff-capable session followed the access-failure
      rule after at most one logged presentation-check correction per target URL. No
