./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1164-  and once installed that code can erase the bootloader — `o2p_flash_program`
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1165-  is generic and the boot pages are not write-protected. The closing moves, in
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1166-  order of cost: flash write-protection (WRP) on pages 0–7, a product and
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1167-  hardware-revision word inside the image that the loader checks, an
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1168-  anti-rollback version counter, and a signature. **None of this is a reason to
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1169-  delay potting** — the alternative to a weakly authenticated updater is no
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1170-  updater — but it should be decided before probes are buried somewhere a
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1171-  stranger can reach the cable.
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md:1172:- **Update commands are executed when broadcast.** A broadcast (address 0)
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1173-  FC16 to the command register reaches every probe at once: one frame can put
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1174-  the whole cable into `ERASING`, or reboot all of it. The dispatcher does not
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md:1175:  tell the register handlers that a frame was a broadcast; it should, and the
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1176-  update block should refuse them.
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1177-- **A page erase can lose a request that arrives during it.** `main()` checks
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1178-  only that no response is transmitting before erasing. Reception is
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1179-  interrupt-driven with no DMA or FIFO, so a frame that begins arriving during
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1180-  the 22–40 ms erase can overrun and vanish. The master sees a timeout and
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1181-  retries, which is survivable — but `tools/o2p_update.py` does not retry
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1182-  transient errors while polling for the erase to finish, so it gives up
./firmware/FIRMWARE.md-1183-  early. Fix the tool first; it is the cheaper half.
